{"title":"River at Dawn","details":"The water carries a soft amber light, still holding last night’s moon while the sun lifts above the eastern hills. I walk the path that curves with the bend, boots light on packed earth, breath rising in small white puffs. A heron stands motionless in the shallows, watching minnows flash silver through the clear glass of the stream. I pause on the wooden footbridge, feel the gentle sway beneath my weight, and listen to the hush of water over stone. The air smells of wet moss and distant wood smoke, cool against my cheeks. Somewhere upstream, a single duck calls once, a low note that echoes into the morning. I think of nothing in particular, only let the senses report: the faint taste of last night’s coffee on my tongue, the sound of my own heartbeat matching the slow thud of hooves from a distant farm, the sight of light spilling across the water like spilled copper. I turn back when the sun is high enough to warm my shoulders, carrying the quiet with me like a hidden stone in my pocket."}
